IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wa-interpretation-time`

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (Western Australia) -- PART IV: PROVISIONS AS TO ENACTMENT AND OPERATION OF WRITTEN LAW
-- (ss 28 to 32).

§ `Part IV -- Provisions as to enactment and operation of written law`

§§ `28. Acts deemed public Acts`

-- Section 28: every Act passed after 13 April 1853 is deemed a public Act unless the contrary is expressly
-- provided in the Act, and is judicially noticed as such.  An Act passed on or before 13 April 1853, or one that
-- expressly provides the contrary, is not dealt with by this section (NOTHING).
-- src:420 | Every Act passed after 13 April 1853 shall be deemed to be a public Act unless the contrary is expressly provided in the Act and shall be judicially noticed as such.
@ref s 28 (src:420)
GIVEN `the day the Act was passed` IS A DATE
      `the Act expressly provides that it is not a public Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`an Act is deemed a public Act and judicially noticed as such` `the day the Act was passed` `the Act expressly provides that it is not a public Act` MEANS
    IF     Day `the day the Act was passed` GREATER THAN Day (Date 13 4 1853)
       AND (NOT `the Act expressly provides that it is not a public Act`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `29. Sections to be substantive enactments`

-- Section 29: every section of an Act takes effect as a substantive enactment without introductory words.  Whether
-- the section opens with introductory words makes no difference.
-- src:422 | Every section of an Act takes effect as a substantive enactment without introductory words.
@ref s 29 (src:422)
GIVEN `the section has introductory words` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a section of an Act takes effect as a substantive enactment` `the section has introductory words` MEANS TRUE

§§ `30. Act may be amended in same session`

-- Section 30: an Act may be amended or repealed in the same session of Parliament as that in which it was passed.
-- src:424 | An Act may be amended or repealed in the same session of Parliament as that in which it was passed.
@ref s 30 (src:424)
GIVEN `the amendment or repeal is in the same session as the one in which the Act was passed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an Act may be amended or repealed` `the amendment or repeal is in the same session as the one in which the Act was passed` MEANS TRUE

§§ `31. Preambles, schedules etc. to form part of written law`

DECLARE `Part of the printed text of a written law` IS ONE OF
    `a preamble`
    `an appendix`
    `a schedule`
    `a table`
    `notes to an appendix, schedule or table`
    `a Part heading`
    `a division heading`
    `a subdivision heading`
    `a marginal note`
    `a footnote`
    `a heading to a section, regulation, rule, local law, bylaw or clause, or to a portion of one`

-- Section 31: the preamble forms part of the written law; an appendix or schedule to, or a table in, a written law,
-- together with its notes, forms part of the written law.
-- src:426 | (1) The preamble to a written law forms part of the written law and shall be construed as a part thereof intended to assist in explaining its purport and object.
-- src:427 | (2) An appendix or schedule to or a table in a written law, together with any notes thereto, forms part of the written law.
@ref s 31(1) (src:426)
@ref s 31(2) (src:427)
GIVEN x IS A `Part of the printed text of a written law`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`forms part of the written law under section 31` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `a preamble` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `an appendix` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a schedule` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a table` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `notes to an appendix, schedule or table` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- Section 31(1): the preamble is construed as part of the written law intended to assist in explaining its
-- purport and object.
@ref s 31(1) (src:426)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a preamble is construed as intended to assist in explaining the written law's purport and object` MEANS TRUE

§§ `32. Headings, marginal notes and footnotes`

-- Section 32(1) and (2).  The headings of Parts, divisions and subdivisions form part of the written law.  A
-- marginal note or footnote is taken not to be part of it.  A heading to a section (or regulation, rule, local law,
-- bylaw or clause, or to a portion of one) is taken not to be part of it "in a context where there is no marginal
-- note with respect to the relevant provision"; where there IS a marginal note, the text does not say (NOTHING;
-- fork F8 in NOTES.md).
-- src:429 | (1) The headings of the Parts, divisions and subdivisions into which a written law is divided form part of the written law.
-- src:430 | (2) A marginal note or footnote to a written law and, in a context where there is no marginal note with respect to the relevant provision and notwithstanding subsection (1), a heading to a section, regulation, rule, local law, bylaw, or clause of a written law, or to a portion of a section, regulation, rule, local law, bylaw or clause of a written law, shall be taken not to be part of the written law.
@ref s 32(1) (src:429)
@ref s 32(2) (src:430)
GIVEN x IS A `Part of the printed text of a written law`
      `there is a marginal note with respect to the relevant provision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`forms part of the written law under section 32` x `there is a marginal note with respect to the relevant provision` MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN `a Part heading` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a division heading` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a subdivision heading` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a marginal note` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `a footnote` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `a heading to a section, regulation, rule, local law, bylaw or clause, or to a portion of one` THEN
        IF `there is a marginal note with respect to the relevant provision` THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING
